{"step_index":28,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-11-18T07:59:22Z","input_tokens":84118,"cache_read_tokens":0,"output_tokens":7579,"thinking":"The initial `cx` offset of +0.21 arose from a disparity in the track segment directions. Eastbound segments, comprising 40.3% of the track, contributed to the positive cosine. Westbound segments, only 17% of the track, created a negative contribution. This suggests an asymmetry in the track's directional distribution, biasing the initial guess.\n\n\nThe heading trace's `cx = +0.21` highlights a critical geometric relationship. The track turns from West through a bowl to East.  If the bowl rotates the track to an Eastward heading, after which it stays East for the majority of the lap, the initial Eastward bias is easily explained. However, the track must have some Westward segments to counteract this strong East bias. The analysis will focus on how the carousel's rotation, combined with the track's directional distribution, yields a cosine integral of zero.\n\n\nThe track's shape strongly suggests a balanced loop with a significant \"bowl\" turn. The \"Harbor Peninsula Hook\" configuration, similar to certain race circuits, offers a clear geometric explanation.  The \"bowl\" can rotate the track westward or eastward, but the entire race must return to a heading from which it started. The analysis will focus on how this shape accounts for the observed `cx` offset.\n\n\nThe initial hypothesis regarding the bowl's direction now shifts focus toward how the net heading change interacts with the observed track geometry. Counter-clockwise circuits, which equate to `+360°`, require the bowl to be a right-hand turn of `-188°`. This right-hand bowl within a counter-clockwise loop directly accounts for the observed cosine offset. This insight suggests a track design analogous to the Monaco Portier section or Hockenheim's Sachs-Kurve.\n\n\nThe track's distinct segments point to a specific geometric layout. Analyzing the track, including the steep climb of the `eau_rouge_climb`, and the sharp turns of the \"sky_bridge\", reveals a complex series of directional changes. A counter-clockwise stadium loop with an inward-curving right-hand bowl, mirroring certain race course configurations, seems most plausible. This suggests how the bowl, in conjunction with the loop, shapes the cosine integral.\n\n\nThe `sky_bridge`'s westward trajectory and the potential for a left-hand bowl suggest an alternative. Investigating if the `188°` carousel can be a left-hand turn, the track could utilize an inward-curving bowl. The `188°` carousel bowl's placement, either as an outward peninsula loop or an infield horseshoe, requires detailed coordinate tracing. Initial checks include adjusting the right-hand bowl assumption to a left-hand assumption.\n\n\nThe previous analysis considered a right-hand bowl based on the desired track shape, but the text explicitly details a left-hand, +188° carousel bowl with positive `bank_deg` values. The confusion arose from the prior assumption about the relationship between turn direction and `bank_deg` sign, warranting a re-evaluation of how this configuration affects the cosine integral. This apparent contradiction suggests the existing hypothesis regarding bowl direction needs correction. The analysis must accurately map `bank_deg` to track turning direction within the loop.\n\n\nThe key insight is that a 180° turn always reverses direction, regardless of the initial heading or the direction of the turn. This principle is fundamental to understanding how the bowl interacts with the loop geometry. Applying this to the track's design, a 185° bowl with an initial north direction results in a south direction.\n\n\nThe track's trajectory reveals an Eastward bias followed by a significant Westward shift, leading into the critical carousel bowl. The `188°` clockwise rotation from a Northward entry, with a heading of `+85°`, results in a South-South-West exit heading of `-103°`. A thorough re-tracing of the track's directional segments will be necessary, particularly the canyon chicane, to ensure this heading shift accurately reflects the track's design. The analysis needs to integrate this complete loop to determine if this new understanding corrects prior assumptions about the geometry.\n\n\nThe track's data reveals an unexpected shift in the carousel bowl's behavior. A right-hand turn from an initial NNE heading should result in an eastward trajectory, which contradicts the expected westward heading. The model requires re-tracing the track segments to understand how the right-hand `188°` bowl achieves the westward direction, since the heading must shift from an entry point to a West direction.\n\n\n\nThe existing analysis indicated confusion regarding the carousel bowl's direction based on the observed heading shifts. Clarifying, a left turn of +188° from Northwest results in a West-to-South heading, while a right turn of -188° from West goes to North-to-East. A new approach involves using optimization to clarify the track geometry via a \"Peninsula Clockwise Loop\" analysis. This loop, with a net -360° heading change, suggests that with a -188° corner (carousel bowl), all other corners only require -172°. Initial results showed an incorrect initial heading which did not turn West before the Bowl, explaining why the initial script failed.\n\n\nThe previous calculation, using the assumed -188° carousel, skipped over the crucial Westward heading, resulting in a consistently positive cosine.  A correct heading requires that the `sky_bridge` points West and the carousel ensures it's Westward at a specific segment. Now the analysis shifts focus to whether the assumed geometries of -165° West for the Skybridge and -195° West for the strobe_tunnel meet track requirements. This must include checking how the track can spend 40% of the time heading West with the correct cosine geometry, considering a clockwise bowl.\n\n\nThe track's current trajectory suggests the sky_bridge is oriented west-south-west, around -160 to -130 degrees, representing 17% of the lap with a negative cosine. The right-hand 188° carousel bowl is key. A west-south-west heading (-110°) enters the bowl, transitioning through west to north and then exits at approximately +62° (NNE). Next, the analysis investigates if the corkscrew s-plunge and strobe tunnel geometries align with the overall direction, noting that an S-Plunge turn from a starting angle of +62 degrees, with a leftward +95°, yields a -30° and cosine less than zero. Initial analysis has found a potential problem. This will need further analysis. The cliffside harbor hairpin provides a final crucial turn from a North West heading to an Eastward finish.\n\n\n\nThe current track trace shows an inaccurate heading at the carousel bowl exit. The bowl geometry, originating West-South-West at -115 degrees, with a -188° right-hand turn, should yield an exit heading around +57 degrees (NNE), not an initial result of +303. Detailed re-calculation will verify the intermediate steps of the turn to ensure it aligns with the expected exit trajectory. The analysis will investigate this to ensure the turning geometry, from the canyon section through to the strobe tunnel.\n\n\n","tool_calls":[{"name":"write_file","args":{"Content":"# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-\nimport math\nimport numpy as np\nfrom scipy.optimize import root\n\ndef smooth_bump(u, u0, u1, edge_frac=0.35):\n    w = (u1 - u0) % 1.0\n    du = (u - u0) % 1.0\n    out = np.zeros_like(u, dtype=float)\n    e = max(1e-6, min(0.499, edge_frac)) * w\n    m_rise = (du > 0.0) & (du < e)\n    x = du[m_rise] / e\n    out[m_rise] = x**3 * (10.0 - 15.0 * x + 6.0 * x * x)\n    m_flat = (du >= e) & (du <= w - e)\n    out[m_flat] = 1.0\n    m_fall = (du > w - e) & (du < w)\n    x = (w - du[m_fall]) / e\n    out[m_fall] = x**3 * (10.0 - 15.0 * x + 6.0 * x * x)\n    return out\n\ndef corner_turn(u, u0, u1, angle_deg, edge_frac=0.35):\n    b = smooth_bump(u, u0, u1, edge_frac=edge_frac)\n    integ = np.sum(b) / len(u)\n    return b * (math.radians(angle_deg) / max(integ, 1e-12))\n\nM = 8000\nu = np.linspace(0.0, 1.0, M, endpoint=False)\ndu = 1.0 / M\n\ndef build_xy_unit(params):\n    a_raid, a_bk, a_hp = params\n    # Fixed artistic corners + 3 solver closure corners:\n    corners = [\n        # Zone 1: canyon_grid (0.00 .. 0.16)\n        # 0.00 .. 0.090: Fast urban canyon straight (0 deg East)\n        (0.090, 0.120, -48.0, 0.38),   # Tight Chicane Right\n        (0.118, 0.150, +46.0, 0.38),   # Tight Chicane Left\n        # Zone 2: eau_rouge_climb (0.16 .. 0.33)\n        (0.162, 0.200, +32.0, 0.42),   # Eau-Rouge bottom compression Left\n        (0.198, 0.292, a_raid, 0.40),  # Raidillon sweeping uphill Right (~ -155 deg)\n        (0.290, 0.328, +18.0, 0.45),   # Crest Left flick onto Sky-Bridge\n        # Zone 3: sky_bridge (0.33 .. 0.50)\n        (0.390, 0.450, a_bk,  0.45),   # Fast Sky-Bridge aero kink (~ -20..+20 deg)\n        # Zone 4: banked_bowl_plunge (0.50 .. 0.70)\n        (0.500, 0.596, -188.0, 0.28),  # 188-deg Right-Hand Banked Carousel Bowl!\n        (0.600, 0.648, +98.0, 0.38),   # Laguna-Seca Corkscrew blind Left plunge!\n        (0.646, 0.692, -32.0, 0.42),   # Corkscrew lower Right sweep into tunnel!\n        # Zone 5: strobe_tunnel_harbor (0.70 .. 1.00)\n        (0.715, 0.845, +14.0, 0.46),   # Gentle flat-out Cyber-Strobe Tunnel curve\n        (0.855, 0.882, +16.0, 0.44),   # Blinding exit cliffside positioning bend\n        (0.896, 0.922, a_hp,  0.34),   # Heavy-Braking Cliffside Harbor Hairpin (~ -125 deg)\n    ]\n    kap = np.zeros_like(u)\n    for u0, u1, ang, ef in corners:\n        kap += corner_turn(u, u0, u1, ang, ef)\n\n    theta = np.cumsum(kap) * du\n    theta -= theta[0]\n    total_dtheta = np.sum(kap) * du\n    cx = np.sum(np.cos(theta)) * du\n    cy = np.sum(np.sin(theta)) * du\n    return kap, theta, np.array([total_dtheta + 2.0 * math.pi, cx, cy])\n\nsol = root(lambda p: build_xy_unit(p)[2], x0=[-155.0, 15.0, -125.0], method='hybr', tol=1e-14)\nprint(\"Solver success:\", sol.success, \"params (deg):\", sol.x, \"residual:\", sol.fun)\n\nkap_u, theta_u, res = build_xy_unit(sol.x)\nx_u = np.cumsum(np.cos(theta_u)) * du\ny_u = np.cumsum(np.sin(theta_u)) * du\nx_u -= x_u[0]\ny_u -= y_u[0]\n\nL2D = 3630.0\nX = x_u * L2D\nY = y_u * L2D\nKAP = kap_u / L2D\n\nprint(f\"End closure gap XY: {math.hypot(X[-1] + math.cos(theta_u[-1])*du*L2D, Y[-1] + math.sin(theta_u[-1])*du*L2D):.3e} m\")\nprint(f\"BBox X: [{X.min():.1f}, {X.max():.1f}] ({X.max()-X.min():.1f} m), Y: [{Y.min():.1f}, {Y.max():.1f}] ({Y.max()-Y.min():.1f} m)\")\nprint(f\"Max |curv_cl|: {np.abs(KAP).max():.5f} (min R_cl = {1.0/np.abs(KAP).max():.2f} m)\")\n\nsub = 10\nXs, Ys = X[::sub], Y[::sub]\nMs = len(Xs)\ns_sub = np.arange(Ms) * (L2D / Ms)\nfor thresh in (100.0, 120.0, 140.0, 160.0):\n    min_sep = 1e9\n    min_pair = None\n    for i in range(Ms):\n        ds_cyc = np.minimum((s_sub - s_sub[i]) % L2D, (s_sub[i] - s_sub) % L2D)\n        dxy = np.hypot(Xs - Xs[i], Ys - Ys[i])\n        m = dxy[ds_cyc >= thresh]\n        if len(m) and m.min() < min_sep:\n            j = np.where(ds_cyc >= thresh)[0][np.argmin(m)]\n            min_sep = float(m.min())\n            min_pair = (round(float(s_sub[i]/L2D), 3), round(float(s_sub[j]/L2D), 3))\n    print(f\"Min XY sep (|ds| >= {thresh:.0f}m): {min_sep:.2f} m at s_frac={min_pair}\")\n","Overwrite":true,"Path":"C:\\Users\\86052\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\62527b59-0213-41b2-b3ab-ff115c915c3b\\scratch\\design_circuit.py"}},{"name":"run_command","args":{"CommandLine":"python \"C:\\Users\\86052\\.gemini\\antigravity\\brain\\62527b59-0213-41b2-b3ab-ff115c915c3b\\scratch\\design_circuit.py\"","WaitMsBeforeAsync":5000,"toolSummary":"Test closed C-shaped harbor loop solver"}}]}
